Example 1. Failure to State a Claim: This defense argues that even if all the allegations in the complaint are true, the plaintiff has not presented a legal theory that entitles them to a legal remedy. This can be a powerful defense if elements of the plaintiff's claims are not met.  **(See Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(6) (requiring a plausible claim for relief))**

Example 2. Laches: This defense could claim that Digital Envoy unreasonably delayed in bringing the claim, to the detriment of Google. The concept of laches is based on the principle that equity aids the vigilant and not those who slumber on their rights.  **(See Danjaq LLC v. Sony Corp., 263 F.3d 942, 952 (9th Cir. 2001) (requiring unreasonable delay plus prejudice))**

